ICML-2023-P0424,ICML-2023-A001866,Daniel Furelos-Blanco,stacked,2,5,5,"Hierarchical reinforcement learning (HRL; Barto & Mahadevan, ) frameworks, such as options (Sutton et al., ), have been used to exploit RMs by learning policies at two levels of abstraction: (i) select a formula (i.e., subgoal) from a given RM state, and (ii) select an action to (eventually) satisfy the chosen formula (Toro Icarte et al., ; Furelos-Blanco et al., ). The subtask decomposition powered by HRL enables learning at multiple scales simultaneously, and eases the handling of long-horizon and sparse reward tasks. In addition, several works have considered the problem of learning the RMs themselves from interaction (e.g., Toro Icarte et al., ; Xu et al., ; Furelos-Blanco et al., ; Hasanbeig et al., ). A common problem among methods learning minimal RMs is that they scale poorly as the number of states grows.",-6,data/raw/icml/2023/first_pages/ICML-2023-P0424.txt,https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/furelos-blanco23a/furelos-blanco23a.pdf
